 - CertificateVerify is an ECDSA P-256 signature over the SHA-256 transcript
-  hash as is (prehashed). The sensor only accepts **exactly 70 bytes**:
-  `30 44 02 20 <r 32> 02 20 <s 32>` with no extra zero byte when the top
-  bit is set. DER from openssl is 71 or 72 bytes when r or s has the top
-  bit set (about half of the handshakes in testing) and those fail with alert 20 (bad
-  record mac) on the Finished. With the fixed layout 25 of 25 worked.
+  hash as is (prehashed), the body is the plain DER signature. Its length
+  varies, 69 to 72 bytes, with a leading zero byte in front of an r or s
+  with the top bit set. The vendor driver sent 70 bytes (`30 44 ...`) in
+  three handshakes of the traces and 72 bytes (`30 46 ...`, r and s 33
+  bytes each) in the other three, the sensor took all of them. 30
+  consecutive open, list, close cycles with the driver (69 bytes once, 70
+  four times, 71 seventeen times, 72 eight times) all went through. An
+  earlier version of these notes said only a fixed 70 byte form works, that
+  was wrong. The failures seen with it (alert 20 on the Finished) had
+  another cause, not the signature.
 - Transcript hash is SHA-256 over all handshake messages (4 byte header
   included, as received/sent), the Finished messages are **not** part of
   it, so client and server verify data use the same hash.
