-## Open points for the hardware test
-
-- Does the sensor take a non-zero tail in the identity (user id string).
-- Does cleanup change anything but the deleted counters.
-- Duplicate check: the driver identifies once before the enroll (extra
-  touch, tested by tudor-owner-session2.sh part A). The vendor trace has
-  no command for it: the engine's CheckForDuplicate in synaFpAdapter132.dll
-  calls the same internal identify as verify and expects NO_MATCH, but
-  cross-enroll.trace shows no 0x99 between the last 0x96 02 and the
-  commit. The only host side duplicate check seen (identity already in
-  the record store, WINBIO_E_DUPLICATE_ENROLLMENT) is keyed by identity,
-  not by finger. Whether identify works inside an open enroll session is
-  still untested, it would save the extra touch.
+## Hardware results
+
+Owner session 1 (20261029-182227-*): list, delete of an unknown id
+(DATA_NOT_FOUND), cancelled identify, finger status flags, a cancelled
+enroll that stores nothing, a full enroll, metadata round trip (user name,
+finger, date), identify, delete and the cleanup all worked. The cleanup ran
+at the start of an enroll on a sensor with 0 free slots and the enroll then
+succeeded. One check failed, a verify with the other enrolled finger
+that was expected to not match the test print. A focused crossmatch
+(20261029-183701-crossmatch.log) identified the left index 5 of 5 times
+as the left index, so the failed check was most likely the wrong finger on
+the sensor. Delete refuses unless the parent query agrees on the user, and
+it worked, so the user id offset in the object info reply is right.
+
+Owner session 2 (20261029-221359-session2*): enrolling the left index again
+was refused with DATA_DUPLICATE after the first touch and stored nothing.
+An enroll with odd touches (fingertip, very light, edge of the sensor)
+finished without a reported retry, the sensor took all of them. Identify
+and a full enroll, verify and identify worked after the trimmed open.
+
+Other findings from the traces and logs:
+
+- Duplicate check: the driver identifies once before the enroll (one extra
+  touch). The vendor trace has no command for it: the engine's
+  CheckForDuplicate in synaFpAdapter132.dll calls the same internal
+  identify as verify and expects NO_MATCH, but cross-enroll.trace shows no
+  0x99 between the last 0x96 02 and the commit. The only host side
+  duplicate check seen (identity already in the record store,
+  WINBIO_E_DUPLICATE_ENROLLMENT) is keyed by identity, not by finger.
 - Rejects: the frame event only has bit 31 of the flags, nothing else was
   ever set. The vendor "reject detail 0x7" is WinBio POOR_QUALITY, a
   constant for a flagged frame, so too short / off centre cannot be told
-  apart and the driver says RETRY_GENERAL. A very short tap was accepted
-  by the sensor (owner session 1, B8). Part B of the second session logs
-  the flags of odd touches.
-- A 0x96 02 reply whose capture mask did not grow (cross-enroll.trace has
-  one, mask 3 twice) is a capture the sensor took without using. The driver
-  reports it as a retry, not a stage.
-- Open sequence: trimmed to version, pairing and TLS, see above. The old
-  FP_TUDOR_OPEN_TRIM knob is gone.
-- Duplicate check and rejects, owner session 2: enrolling the left index
-  again was refused after the first touch and stored nothing. Odd touches
-  (tip, very light, edge) in an enroll gave no reject and no retry, the
-  sensor took them all.
-- Is the user id offset in the object info reply right (the driver
-  refuses to delete unless the parent filter query agrees).
-- What a rejected capture does in an enroll.
-- A trace of listing the data of an object would allow list to give real
-  prints.
+  apart and the driver says RETRY_GENERAL. A very short tap and the odd
+  touches of session 2 were all accepted.
+- A 0x96 02 reply whose capture mask did not grow (cross-enroll.trace and
+  the crossmatch enroll each have one, stage 3 twice) is a capture the
+  sensor took without using. The driver reports it as a retry, not a stage.
+- Open sequence: version, pairing and TLS only, see above.
+- The a1 object data read works, so list returns real prints.
+
+## Debug environment
+
+`FP_TUDOR_PAIRING_DIR` moves the pairing directory. `FP_TUDOR_NEVER_REPAIR`
+(dev branch only, not upstream) makes an open fail where it would pair the
+sensor again, also when the sensor refuses the stored pairing, so a test
+run can never orphan the enrolled templates.
+
+## Still open
+
+- Whether identify works inside an open enroll session, it would save the
+  extra touch of the duplicate check.
+- What a rejected capture does in the middle of an enroll (no touch was
+  rejected so far, only the vendor trace has a flagged frame, in a verify).
+- Whether cleanup changes anything but the counters (slots were freed).
 
